WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.399
Authentication and authorization are
different controls,

2
00:00:06.479 --> 00:00:12.609
and secure Axum applications keep that
distinction explicit.

3
00:00:12.689 --> 00:00:17.662
Authentication answers who is making the
request.

4
00:00:17.742 --> 00:00:26.070
Authorization answers whether that
identity may perform the requested action on the

5
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.232
requested resource.

6
00:00:28.312 --> 00:00:35.714
A route can know the user and still make
the wrong decision if it does not check

7
00:00:35.794 --> 00:00:41.230
ownership, role, tenant, scope, or
operation context.

8
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:47.556
Axum applications may establish identity
through middleware,

9
00:00:47.636 --> 00:00:54.807
request extensions, session layers, token
validation, signed cookies,

10
00:00:54.887 --> 00:01:00.439
upstream identity providers, or dedicated
extractors.

11
00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:06.418
Whatever mechanism is used, the trust
model should be clear.

12
00:01:06.498 --> 00:01:13.437
Reviewers should know where identity is
accepted, how it is validated,

13
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:16.178
how expiration is handled,

14
00:01:16.258 --> 00:01:22.503
and how missing or invalid identity is
represented to handlers.

15
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:30.101
Authorization should be checked close to
the protected resource and action.

16
00:01:30.181 --> 00:01:35.155
Administrative routes need explicit
protection.

17
00:01:35.235 --> 00:01:40.208
Tenant-specific operations need tenant
context.

18
00:01:40.288 --> 00:01:45.725
Object-level actions need ownership or
policy checks.

19
00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:52.397
A common mistake is to assume that because
a route is authenticated,

20
00:01:52.477 --> 00:01:57.913
the user can safely act on any identifier
in the request.

21
00:01:57.993 --> 00:02:02.273
That assumption should not survive review.

22
00:02:02.353 --> 00:02:08.599
Sessions and tokens require defensive
handling at a high level.

23
00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:13.652
They should not be logged, reflected in
responses,

24
00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:17.433
or treated as plain application data.

25
00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:25.957
Expiration, rotation, invalidation, and
scope decisions should be understood by the

26
00:02:26.037 --> 00:02:26.615
team.

27
00:02:26.695 --> 00:02:33.403
If an upstream proxy or identity service
supplies identity headers,

28
00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:41.695
the Axum application should only trust
those headers under a documented deployment

29
00:02:41.775 --> 00:02:42.816
boundary.

30
00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:46.482
Fail-closed behavior is essential.

31
00:02:46.562 --> 00:02:52.345
If identity is absent, malformed, expired,
inconsistent,

32
00:02:52.425 --> 00:02:59.943
or missing required authorization context,
the handler should not proceed.

33
00:03:00.023 --> 00:03:05.690
The failure should be predictable, safe,
and observable.

34
00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:13.867
Secure design makes the allowed path
explicit and treats uncertainty as a reason to

35
00:03:13.947 --> 00:03:16.260
stop rather than guess.
